id=3D"content"><article class=3D"style_learnContent__K5K7M"><header><h1>Low=
est Common Ancestor</h1></header><div class=3D"style_problemDescription__T4=
cPe"><p>Return the lowest common ancestor (LCA) of two nodes, <code>p</code=
> and <code>q</code>, in a binary tree. The LCA is defined as the <strong>l=
owest node that has both <code>p</code> and <code>q</code> as descendants</=
strong>. A node can be considered an ancestor of itself.</p><h4 id=3D"examp=
le">Example:</h4><figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justif=
y-content: center;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a binary tree structure wh=
ere nodes are represented by circles containing numerical labels (1 through=
 9). Node 1 is the root node, branching into nodes 2 and 3. Node 2 further =
branches into nodes 4 and 5. Node 3 branches into nodes 6 and 7. Node 6 fur=
ther branches into nodes 8 and 9.  Node 3 is highlighted in orange and labe=
led 'LCA' (Least Common Ancestor) in orange text above it, indicating its s=
ignificance in the context of finding the LCA within the tree.  Additionall=
y, node 8 is labeled with a 'p' in cyan, and node 7 is labeled with a 'q' i=
n cyan, suggesting these nodes ('p' and 'q') are the target nodes for which=
 the LCA (node 3) is being identified.  The lines connecting the nodes repr=
esent parent-child relationships within the tree hierarchy." width=3D"328" =
height=3D"215" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego=
.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/trees/lowest-common-ancestor/lowest-com=
mon-ancestor-DEL2KVAS.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure><h4=
 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>
<p>The tree contains at least two nodes.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All node values are unique.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><code>p</code> and <code>q</code> represent different nodes in the tree.=
</p>
</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>One strategy to solve this problem is to traverse each node in the tree =
and evaluate whether the current node at any point is the LCA of p and q. T=
o make this evaluation, it=E2=80=99s crucial to know the conditions a node =
needs to meet to be the LCA.</p>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s start by discussing where p or q would need to be for the =
current node to be at least an ancestor of both.</p>
<p><strong>Identifying when the current node is an ancestor of p and q</str=
ong><br>
Consider the following binary tree. Let's explore where p and q would need =
to be for node 3 to be an ancestor.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a binary tree structure where nodes are rep=
