<p>As we can see above, there are three possible cases where node 3 is the =
LCA. This is because in each case, there isn=E2=80=99t a node lower than no=
de 3 that contains both p and q as descendants, making node 3 the LCA.</p>
<p>Note that if both p and q were in just one of node 3=E2=80=99s subtrees =
(e.g., the left subtree), node 3 would not be the LCA because there would b=
e a node lower in the left subtree that=E2=80=99s an ancestor of both p and=
